/*
  Psychiatric Interviewing Trainer — Study guide, Chapter 12: Interviewing Techniques for Understanding the Person Beneath the Psychosis
  Written in our own words as a companion to Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.).
  Clinical exchanges are composed for teaching; they are not transcripts from the book. Book cases are summarized.
  Schema: see guide-ch1.js.
*/
window.TRAINER = window.TRAINER || { parts: {} };
window.TRAINER.guides = window.TRAINER.guides || {};
window.TRAINER.guides.ch12 = {
  chapter: "ch12",
  intro: "Chapter 11 taught the diseases that produce psychosis; Chapter 12 is about the person living inside one. Shea's epigraph is Poe's Roderick Usher expecting to lose life and reason together in a struggle with fear, and fear is the thread: psychosis spreads through every wing of the matrix like a virus, and the interviewer who understands that terror from the inside asks better questions and hears more. The chapter walks the five wings again. In the biological wing you learn sleep as an early warning, Schneider's first-rank symptoms as the inventory of a 'porous ego', akathisia, and how to approach a catatonic patient. The psychological wing is the heart of the chapter: how to raise voices without disengaging anyone, how to explore their phenomenology, how to assess command hallucinations, and what the deficit symptoms do to a person's sense of self. The dyadic wing covers the precox feeling, agreeing to disagree, and your own effect on the patient; the family wing covers mourning, denial, the alliance with parents, and confidentiality; the cultural material includes koro, the Hmong, euphemisms, and a Filipino patient whose delusions existed only in Ilocano. The worldview wing ends with Jake, whose last delusion was the one that held him up. The practice items, the first-rank drill, and the simulation 'Another voice' are built on this chapter.",

  highYield: {
    mustKnow: [
      "Sleep goes first. Trouble falling asleep is a sensitive sign of impending psychosis, often appearing during delusional mood or delusional perception; day/night reversal can follow, and early morning awakening when the psychosis belongs to a depression. Patients deny it, so ask the family, who have been awake with them.",
      "The [[Porous ego]]: psychotic patients lose the limits of their bodies and of their 'mineness'. The world seems to invade them, and that one-way invasion is much of the fear. When intense, it brings a terror of annihilation that explains some drastic and violent acts.",
      "Schneider's [[First-rank symptoms]] are eleven phenomena: seven of invasion ([[Somatic passivity]], [[Made symptoms|made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts]], [[Thought withdrawal]], [[Thought insertion]], [[Thought broadcasting]]) plus delusional perception and three kinds of voices ([[Audible thoughts]], voices arguing, voices commenting). Schneider was wrong that they nearly guarantee schizophrenia (they occur in other psychoses, though more often in schizophrenia), but they are an excellent inventory of psychotic experience and belong in every interviewer's repertoire.",
      "Read Schneider as a phenomenologist: what mattered was that the person experienced thought withdrawal and was troubled by it, not whether it is filed as a haptic hallucination or a delusion. And a first-rank symptom counts only inside a psychotic matrix, Schneider's [[Phenomenological leverage]]: a psychotic phenomenon is not a single flawed stone in an otherwise perfect mosaic.",
      "Delusions are often the answer a person finds to an unnatural experience. Something squirms inside me; who is doing it? Parasites. I am made to feel hate; by whom? A demon. The made symptoms and somatic passivity typically predate the delusion, so questions that uncover them can catch a first break or a breakthrough on medication before any delusion exists, and earlier treatment means lower doses and a gentler episode.",
      "Patients will not volunteer these experiences, because they sound crazy; ask directly. Something moving or squirming inside your body? Something or someone making you feel an emotion? Giving you urges you would never normally have? Pulling thoughts out of your mind, or pushing thoughts in through a phone or a neighbor? Others able to hear your thoughts? Unusual powers like sending thoughts to others?",
      "[[Thought leakage]] (passive thought broadcasting, 'thought diffusion') is experienced as the end of all privacy and is usually felt negatively; the rarer intentional sending of thoughts can feel like a special power and a pleasure. The locus differs from thought withdrawal: nothing is being done to me, my thoughts are escaping through me.",
      "[[Akathisia]] is a subjective, deep-seated need to move, caused by typical and atypical antipsychotics, some SSRIs, and two antiemetics. It is easily mistaken for psychotic agitation, it can worsen psychosis and drive self-harm and suicide, and a patient with coexisting drug-induced parkinsonism may feel the intense drive while barely moving. Patients describe a bodily restlessness in the limbs; psychotic agitation usually lacks that. Sometimes only a trial of treatment tells them apart.",
      "Stuporous catatonia occurs in schizophrenia, mood disorders, dissociation, and medical illness including autoimmune encephalitis. Its negative symptoms are mutism, immobility, [[Negativism]], and [[Ambitendency]]; its positive symptoms are posturing, echoing the clinician's words, and [[Waxy flexibility]]. Speak gently and at normal volume, say everything you want the patient to hear (they may be processing it now or later), and do not touch in a first interview; if you must, have someone present, explain who you are and what you will do and why, and be ready to move.",
      "Voices are the commonest hallucination: known or unknown, real or imagined figures, single words to running conversations, sometimes non-verbal (machines, music). About 75% of people with schizophrenia, 20 to 50% with bipolar disorder, 10% with major depression, and up to 40% with PTSD (in flashbacks) hear them. One of Shea's patients used the ice-cream-truck music that preceded his demonic voices as a cue to take a prn dose.",
      "Jaspers was wrong that true hallucinations must sound real and occupy outside space. Voices are heard inside the head, outside, or both (roughly a third each in Copolov's study), with no difference in severity or distress; the DSM-5 accepts all of them. Internal voices go with somewhat better reality testing, yet patients who hear commands only externally resist them better. Internal voices make dissociative identity disorder unlikely, and DID voices are not embedded in a psychotic matrix.",
      "Voices are veridical (felt as real) but patients can often tell them from real voices and may name them. Malingerers describe voices exactly like real ones that started suddenly on a good day, and miss the hostile, insulting quality typical of schizophrenia. An [[Auditory illusion]] distorts a real sound; a [[Functional hallucination]] is triggered by a real sound and heard alongside it.",
      "Raise voices in a way that ties them to the patient's pain and offers a back door: when you are feeling very depressed (or anxious, stressed, upset), do your thoughts ever get so intense that they sound almost like a voice? Or the SCAN question about hearing noises or voices with nobody about and no ordinary explanation. If a delusion is already on the table, hook the question to it: do you ever hear Fred's voice when he isn't in the room? Screen every patient; fluctuation and micropsychotic episodes mean a normal-looking interview proves nothing, and the rare patient who balks is telling you something about their defenses.",
      "Explore the phenomenology (sound, realness, location, loudness, frequency, identity, name, content, taunts and kindness, realness in the patient's view, wish to keep or lose them, what they do to stop them, what they first thought the voices were, feelings while hearing them, voices talking to each other), over several sessions if need be. Doing it well deepens engagement, and changes in phenomenology (quieter, less frequent, moving inward, more distance) are often the first sign a treatment is working. Once voices are present, always ask about commands.",
      "[[Command hallucination|Command hallucinations]] range from 'shut the door' to 'cut your eye out' and 'push him in front of the train'; voices also tell patients not to listen to you. Ask simply (do your voices ever tell you to do things? what?), then specifically about hurting or killing self and others, as Kenney's 'hurt yourself by failing the test' shows that the first answer is not always the dangerous one. Old chart studies found no link with violence; Shawyer found that acting on commands goes with older age, believing the command will do good, and a tight fit with a delusional system (antipsychotics protective), and Junginger found eight of twenty patients with dangerous commands had acted on them. Statistics do not tell you what this patient will do tonight.",
      "Four dimensions of dangerousness: content; auditory quality (loudness, repetition, incessant frequency, insistence, though a whisper from a revered source can suffice); the patient's felt ability to resist (ask how far they can stop themselves, how worried they are, whether you or the family should worry, and what they have done to stop themselves, like taping the knives in a box in the attic); and emotional impact, including the authority of the voice's owner (a parent, a president, God, Satan) and its viciousness. A benevolent-sounding command inside a delusion ('world peace if you slay your child'), a malevolent harangue that drives suicide to escape it, plus alcohol, drugs, or sleeplessness, make an imminent emergency.",
      "Thought process: racing, contagious associations that branch in ten directions, derailment in speech, and [[Thought blocking]], which strongly suggests psychosis; ask quietly what just happened, because it is often a voice or thought withdrawal. A patient hallucinating during the interview may attribute the voice's insults to you; that is when violence toward the interviewer can erupt. [[Paleologic|Predicative logic]] (Christ was persecuted; I am persecuted; I am Christ) and magical thinking vary in degree.",
      "With an agitated psychotic patient, do not decide in advance whether they can be talked down. Engage gently and gauge the logic; if intact, talk; if reasoning raises the agitation, or the logic is plainly broken, or you have been built into the delusion, stop defending yourself, back off, and follow safety procedures. The patient is not processing your words.",
      "Enter paranoia and other psychotic regions through natural, implied, and referred gates keyed to what the patient is saying (an ugly marriage: has he tried to hurt you, turn your friends against you? nightmares: frightening images by day? talents: unusual abilities like ESP? TV or YouTube: ever felt watched through it? Twitter: tweets aimed at you? a sister's voices: anything similar?). Religion is one of the best gates ('does God have a special role for you?'). A few probes settle whether to expand; do not beat the region into the ground.",
      "The deficit symptoms do the deepest damage: patients read low drive and energy as proof of their own inadequacy, families call it laziness ('the voices are gone, why doesn't he get a job?'), siblings and friends pull ahead in an unwinnable race, and demoralization between episodes is a prelude to suicide. Recovery begins in the first interview: people first, diagnosis second; a person coping with schizophrenia, never 'a schizophrenic'; Deegan's distinction between [[Recovery vs rehabilitation|rehabilitation and recovery]].",
      "The dyadic wing: withdrawal, inappropriate closeness, odd affect, diminished empathy. Your own confusion mirrors the patient's disorganization and is a cue to look harder; the test for formal thought disorder is whether a stranger on the bus would follow, not whether an intuitive clinician can. The [[Precox feeling]], an inability to feel with the patient, is a useful prompt to check schizophrenia's criteria and never a criterion. When the patient rejects your view, [[Agree to disagree]] calmly and leave the door open; heated attempts to convince are counterproductive. Persistent negativism: point out that automatic 'no' surrenders control as much as automatic 'yes'.",
      "You change the picture: lower-valence empathic statements with paranoid patients; ordinary words can carry private meanings; frightened, disorganized patients do better with reassurance of safety, explanation, invitations to ask, and gentle structure (open-ended silence can traumatize); and a skilled empathic interviewer can make subtle psychosis recede so far that it is missed.",
      "Psychosis is a family affair: financial ruin, an adult child at home, stigma, helplessness, caretaker fatigue, the hatred of a relative who is now in the delusion, occasional assault. Families mourn the person they knew, in intermingled stages, and denial is one of them; understanding that softens your countertransference when they undercut an admission. Over-involvement, hostile or anxiously caring, hinders recovery (EPICS); family counseling cuts relapse. Meet them where they are (Murray-Swank), ask what they think is wrong and what has helped, dismantle the unspoken fears, ask about prior professionals, offer NAMI and the family guides, acknowledge the shock of a locked unit, and tell the parent who committed a child that it took courage and may have saved a life.",
      "Confidentiality: ask the patient who counts as family (friends, a pastor, a sponsor included), how close and how often, whether they have been involved, and the benefits and worries of involving them, so the patient makes an informed choice. If permission is refused, soften the exclusion with [[Counterprojection]] ('it's hard for families; it's hard for us too; I'll ask her permission the next chance I get'). Suicide or violence risk overrides confidentiality; consult if you can and document why.",
      "Culture shapes psychosis in both directions. [[Koro]] (fear of genital retraction into the body, sometimes of dying from it) is never volunteered and was found only when asked with a normalization; qi-gong-induced psychosis is recognized in the Chinese classification. Conversely a Hmong patient speaking matter-of-factly of spirits and voices may simply be describing nyuaj siab in a culture without a word for depression and without the West's sharp line between real and imagined; psychosis shows itself by the matrix of prodromal signs around the voices. All diagnoses are [[Culture-bound syndrome|culture-bound]], the DSM's included.",
      "Language can hide psychosis. Takeshita's 79-year-old Filipino patient, admitted after deciding to kill his wife before she killed him, showed no delusions interviewed in English, fragments in Tagalog, and fixed, extensive delusions about many relatives in Ilocano. Re-interview in the patient's exact language or dialect when you suspect psychosis; disorganizing patients regress to their first language; and learn the local euphemisms ('neurasthenia' or 'psychosomatic' in Japan, 'nervous breakdown' in the United States, which usually means an agitated depression or a psychotic or manic episode and often a hospitalization). Patients with chronic illness also join a subculture with its own medication prejudices: ask whether they know anyone on the drug and what people around here think of it.",
      "Through the window of psychosis the world is too meaningful, a desert of burning bushes. Religious preoccupation can become so intense that religion stops being a support, and a grandiose delusion may be propping up a self-esteem that family, work, and friends no longer support, so losing the delusion can mean losing meaning. Jake's last delusion, that he could send his thoughts farther than anyone, dissolved with his recovery; he wept at his bedside, and years later died by suicide. Delusion content carries the person's upbringing, values, culture, and fears; and the shattering of purpose in chronic psychotic illness is one of the roots of the suicidal ideation so common in it."
    ],
    pitfalls: [
      "Asking only about delusions and hallucinations, and never about squirming insides, made feelings, made impulses, thought withdrawal, insertion, or leakage, so the earliest and most treatable phase of a psychosis goes unasked and unanswered.",
      "Treating the presence of a single first-rank symptom as proof of schizophrenia, or of psychosis at all, without the surrounding matrix.",
      "Raising the antipsychotic dose for 'agitation' that is akathisia, and never asking whether the restlessness lives in the limbs.",
      "Raising your voice at a mute catatonic patient, or shaking her shoulder, when she may be hearing everything and may move from stillness to attack in an instant.",
      "Deciding that a patient who hears voices 'inside my head' is not really hallucinating.",
      "Never screening a patient who looks intact, on the theory that the crazy questions will offend; fluctuating and micropsychotic process is missed, and the question seldom harms engagement.",
      "Hearing 'yes' to 'does the voice tell you to hurt yourself?' and stopping there; Kenney's voice told him to fail his exam, and only the specific question about cutting and pills reached 'do it'.",
      "Ignoring a benign command ('change your profile picture') because it is benign, instead of following it in later sessions as it advances toward dangerous content.",
      "Being reassured by statistics that command hallucinations rarely lead to violence, or by a patient's promise alone, when the patient's own fear that they are about to give in is the signal that matters.",
      "Trying to reason an agitated patient out of a delusion that includes you, and raising the temperature until violence erupts.",
      "Understanding what the patient means and therefore not noticing the loosening of associations; the layperson on the bus is the standard.",
      "Spending a session arguing a paranoid patient into accepting the diagnosis, instead of agreeing to disagree and keeping the door open.",
      "Reading the exhausted mother who undercuts the admission as obstructive rather than as mourning in the denial stage.",
      "Telling a frantic parent 'I can't discuss anything because of confidentiality' and nothing else.",
      "Calling the Hmong patient's spirits psychotic, and missing the Chinese father's koro because you did not know to ask.",
      "Interviewing a patient in your language, or in the national language, rather than in theirs, and concluding that there are no delusions.",
      "Congratulating a patient on losing a grandiose delusion without asking what it had been holding up."
    ],
    phrases: [
      { say: "Do you ever feel that something is moving or squirming inside your body?", when: "Screening for somatic passivity, the first-rank symptom most clinicians never think to ask about." },
      { say: "Have you ever felt that something or someone was making you feel an emotion, as if something were making you angry or sad or bitter? Or giving you urges you'd never normally have, like shouting at a stranger or hurting someone?", when: "Made feelings and made impulses, asked directly; patients rarely volunteer them because they sound crazy. Composed." },
      { say: "Does it ever feel as if someone, or something like a demon or the internet, can pull thoughts out of your mind against your will? Or push thoughts into your mind that aren't your own, through your phone, say?", when: "Thought withdrawal and thought insertion; tie the question to whatever agent the patient has already named. Composed." },
      { say: "Are you ever worried that other people can hear or read your thoughts without your knowing, or that your thoughts end up posted somewhere? And has it ever seemed that you can send your thoughts out to other people, even a little?", when: "Both forms of thought broadcasting, the feared leakage and the sometimes welcome power. Composed." },
      { say: "Is the restlessness in your body, in your legs, like you have to move, or is it more in your mind?", when: "Separating akathisia from psychotic agitation before anyone raises the dose. Composed." },
      { say: "It's all right not to talk now. Whenever you feel like talking, please do, and ask any of the staff for me; I'll come as soon as I can.", when: "Said at normal volume to a mute catatonic patient who may be processing every word. Composed." },
      { say: "When you're feeling very stressed, do your thoughts ever get so intense that they sound almost like a voice to you?", when: "Shea's favorite way to raise voices: tied to the patient's pain, with 'like a voice' as a back door. Swap in depressed, anxious, or upset as fits." },
      { say: "Do you ever hear Fred's voice when he isn't actually in the room with you?", when: "Raising hallucinations when a delusion about Fred is already on the table. Composed." },
      { say: "Some people hear their voices only inside their heads, some only outside, some both. How is it for you? Do they sound just like real voices, or can you tell the difference? How?", when: "Opening the phenomenology without implying that one answer is the 'real' one." },
      { say: "Do your voices ever tell you to do things? What do they tell you to do? Do they ever tell you to hurt yourself, or kill yourself? To hurt or kill someone else?", when: "The command-hallucination sequence: general, then specific, then both directions of danger; always asked once voices are present." },
      { say: "How far do you think you can stop yourself from doing what the voice is asking? How worried are you that you'll do it? Should I, or your family, be worried? Have you done anything to stop yourself?", when: "Assessing the ability to resist; the patient's own fear of giving in should be taken very seriously. Composed." },
      { say: "We obviously disagree about whether this is bipolar disorder, and that's all right; we can agree to disagree. I'll always tell you what I really think, and I know you'll do the same. And I could be wrong. Time will help us both sort it out, and I'm here whenever you want to talk again.", when: "Ending a disagreement about the diagnosis without a fight, with the door open. Composed." },
      { say: "Nobody in the world knows him better than you. We depend on what you've seen, including what has worked and what hasn't. And some people think parents cause this illness; I don't believe that for a minute.", when: "Dismantling the unspoken fears of parents in the first family meeting. Composed." },
      { say: "Sometimes people have very private fears about their bodies that are hard to talk about. I know that in some places men worry that the penis is being damaged or drawn up into the body, and even that this could kill them. Have you heard of that, or worried about it yourself?", when: "A normalized question for koro; the fear is never volunteered. Composed after Shea's example." },
      { say: "Which language do you think best in, and which did you grow up speaking at home?", when: "Before concluding there are no delusions in a patient interviewed in a second language or a second dialect. Composed." }
    ]
  },

  sections: [
    {
      id: "porous-ego",
      title: "The biological wing: sleep, the porous ego, and Schneider's first-rank symptoms",
      lede: "An early warning sign, the dissolving limits of the body and self, and the inventory of invasion experiences that most clinicians never ask about.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Sleep is one of the physiological moorings of daily life, and disturbed sleep quickly makes anyone feel not quite themselves, so it is no surprise that sleep disturbance appears early in psychosis. As the process gathers momentum the patient cannot fall asleep; some progress to day/night reversal, sleeping by day and agitated by night; early morning awakening joins in when the psychosis belongs to a major depression. Difficulty falling asleep is a sensitive sign of impending psychosis, often present during [[Delusional mood / perception|delusional mood or perception]], and the patient often denies it, so ask the family, who have usually been awake coping with the restlessness." },
        { type: "p", text: "The next somatic concern is far from ordinary experience. Psychotic patients have trouble determining the limits of their bodies and, in parallel, the limits of the self, their realness, their sense of 'mineness'. The idea is that psychotic process regresses the person toward an infantile state in which the body is part self and part object; depersonalization and derealization intensify until the sense of self or of its autonomy is lost. One patient described limbs that were not his and moved on strings pulled by others, with no boundary between himself and the outside; when intense, such feelings bring a terror of impending annihilation, perhaps the otherworldly dread of Poe's Usher, and that terror helps explain some of the drastic and violent acts of psychotic patients. Shea's phrase for the state is the [[Porous ego]]: the world invades the skin, sensations enter from outside and become one with the person, and the one-directional invasion of their integrity is part of why they are so afraid." },
        { type: "p", text: "Kurt Schneider, pupil of the phenomenologist Jaspers, captured these invasion experiences descriptively. He believed, wrongly, that any of them (absent a medical cause) almost guaranteed schizophrenia, hence [[First-rank symptoms]]; they occur fairly often in other psychoses, though with good evidence that they are more common in schizophrenia than in mood or medical psychoses. Right or wrong about diagnosis, Schneider left an excellent inventory of psychotic phenomena, and questions about them belong in every interviewer's repertoire. Seven are invasion experiences: [[Somatic passivity]], made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts ([[Made symptoms]] together), [[Thought withdrawal]], [[Thought insertion]], and [[Thought broadcasting]]. The other four are delusional perception (Chapter 11) and three forms of auditory hallucination: [[Audible thoughts]], voices arguing, and voices commenting on the patient's actions." },
        { type: "p", text: "The literature is confusing because Schneider never defined the symptoms crisply, and Shea's key to reading him is phenomenology: an interest in how a unique person experiences being in the world, not in filing the experience as a 'thing'. Take thought withdrawal. One patient literally feels a thought leave the skull (a haptic hallucination); another believes thoughts are being withdrawn without feeling it (a delusion with no hallucination). The interpretation varies too: a vague feeling that some unidentified agent did it (a feeling, not a delusion), a vague belief that an outside entity did (an overvalued idea), a definite belief (a delusion), or a specific culprit, the neighbor or a demon (an elaborated delusion). For Schneider what mattered was that the person had experienced thought withdrawal and been concerned about it, in whatever form. And no single symptom made the diagnosis: it had to be embedded in a psychotic matrix, the [[Phenomenological leverage]] he required, because a psychotic phenomenon is not one defective stone in an otherwise perfect mosaic." },
        { type: "table", head: ["First-rank symptom", "What the patient experiences", "How to ask (composed)"], rows: [
          ["Somatic passivity", "Bodily sensations imposed from outside: intestines wriggling, organs shifting; seeds of delusions of twisting insides or infestation", "Do you ever feel that something is moving or squirming inside your body?"],
          ["Made feelings", "An emotion is being put into me; one of Mellor's patients wept while feeling a cold anger that the tears were not his", "Has something or someone ever made you feel an emotion, as if making you angry, sad, or bitter?"],
          ["Made impulses", "Urges that are not mine are being given to me", "Does it sometimes feel as if something is giving you urges you'd never normally have, like shouting at a stranger, swearing, or hurting someone?"],
          ["Made volitional acts", "My body acts without me; a puppet on cosmic strings", "Open: what were you feeling just before you hit him? Closed: have you ever felt that something or someone made you do it?"],
          ["Thought withdrawal", "Thoughts pulled out of the mind by an alien force, sometimes mid-sentence; shows outwardly as thought blocking; paranoid delusions usually follow", "Have you ever felt that a person, or something like a demon or the web, can pull thoughts out of you against your will?"],
          ["Thought insertion", "Thoughts from another entity forced into the mind; often tied to possession or to a computer or phone", "Does Ben ever try to control your thoughts, or literally push his own thoughts into your mind? Have thoughts come into your mind through your phone that aren't yours?"],
          ["Thought broadcasting", "Thoughts leak out for all to hear, even for miles, or are posted on social media; rarely, thoughts can be sent intentionally, felt as a power", "Are you worried that others can hear or read your thoughts? Some people tell me they can send their thoughts to others, even far away; anything like that, even a little?"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "All of the 'made' symptoms share one unifying perception: I am being made to feel, to want, or to do something against my will, including assault or murder, a loss of the innate sense of intentionality that most of us never notice because it simply is. And here is the clinical payoff. It is natural for anyone experiencing a made feeling or a squirming abdomen to ask what is happening to me; the search is normal, and because the sensation is psychotic the answer will be unrealistic: a demon is making me hate, parasites have entered my intestines. Delusions are often the product of a natural hunt for the cause of an unnatural experience, which complements the life cycle of a delusion in Chapter 11 and the idea that a delusion is evidence of an old psychosis. Because the made symptoms and somatic passivity typically predate the delusion, an interviewer who asks about them can detect an insidious first break, or a breakthrough in a patient previously stable on medication, while the patient is still honestly denying delusions. Earlier recognition can mean lower antipsychotic doses and a far less virulent episode." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Why they seem lost in thought", text: "Imagine sorting out, hour by hour, which sensations are real and which are false, as the world leaks in and your thoughts leak out. It takes tremendous attention, which is why these patients so often look preoccupied. To the extent they keep distance from the psychosis they know that much is unreal; as it deepens the distance is lost and the inexplicable becomes a reality that needs no explanation." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "drill", id: "first-rank", label: "Drill: First-rank symptoms" }]
    },

    {
      id: "akathisia-catatonia",
      title: "Akathisia and catatonia",
      lede: "A medication side effect that masquerades as agitation, and how to approach a patient who cannot or will not move.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Antipsychotics have transformed the treatment of psychosis and created new problems in the basal ganglia. Drug-induced parkinsonism blunts the face and is mistaken for the blunting of schizophrenia (Chapter 11). [[Akathisia]] is the second trap: a deep-seated feeling that part or all of the body must move, caused most often by typical and atypical antipsychotics but also by SSRIs and two antiemetics. It usually shows as agitated moving about, sometimes with a small prancing step, but it is a subjective symptom, not a sign; a patient who also has drug-induced parkinsonism may move very little while feeling an intense drive to move, a discordance that is agony. Severe akathisia is a new and bizarre sensation on top of psychosis, and some authors have seen it worsen psychotic states or predispose to self-harm and suicide." },
        { type: "case", title: "The shower", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A young professional, no longer psychotic, described his akathisia: mornings were the worst, because standing under the shower or standing still to dry himself felt impossible, as if he would break out of his skin or be destroyed; at his desk job he could not sit or concentrate and felt ineffectual, certain that everyone would see he was failing; every second was a crisis between having to stay and being unable to; he feared the drug had set off something permanent that would last the rest of his life, grew depressed and panicky, became anxious that others would notice his agitation, and had a resurgence of suicidal thoughts about shooting himself. He had been told about akathisia and its transience, but the psychosis had scrambled the information. Lowering the antipsychotic dose relieved it." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Akathisia is a true bodily sensation: patients describe a need to move and a restlessness in the limbs. Psychotic agitation usually does not come with that.",
          "If there are no other psychotic symptoms that could be driving intense anxiety, akathisia is the likelier explanation.",
          "Sometimes the only way to tell is to treat one or the other and watch.",
          "Interviewing patients with extrapyramidal side effects is a daily event in community mental health, on inpatient units, and in emergency departments; ask about it before anyone raises the dose."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Catatonia filled the back wards of the nineteenth century and is rare now, which is exactly why its interviewing needs rehearsing. Psychotic process can disrupt activity control to either extreme, agitated catatonia (cannot stop moving) or stuporous catatonia (barely moves), and the syndrome is no longer tied to schizophrenia alone; it appears in mood disorders, hysterical dissociation, and medical illnesses including autoimmune encephalitides triggered by infections and cancers. Stuporous catatonia's 'negative symptoms' (not the deficit symptoms of schizophrenia) are mutism, lack of movement, [[Negativism]], and [[Ambitendency]], the hand extended to shake and withdrawn. Its 'positive symptoms' (again not schizophrenia's) are bizarre posturing, senseless repetition of the clinician's words, and [[Waxy flexibility]]. What these patients experience is unknown and probably varies: some are keenly aware of the room, others lost in timelessness." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "Be gentle in tone and calm in pace. The reflex is to speak louder to someone who does not answer; resist it.",
          "Assume the patient may be processing your words, consciously or not, now or later that day. Speak normally and say everything you want communicated: that it is all right not to talk now, that they may talk whenever they feel like it, and how to reach you.",
          "Do not touch in an initial interview. You do not know what the patient is experiencing; if delusional or hallucinating they may perceive attack, and some move from stillness to hyperactivity in an instant (Shea shook the shoulders of an unresponsive woman he feared had overdosed, and she grabbed him and tried to bite him; drugs were not the issue).",
          "If touch is ever judged useful, have a second person present and security aware, tell the patient calmly who you are, what you are about to do, and why, say that they need only ask to be left alone, narrate each move as you go, and be ready to take evasive action."
        ] },
        { type: "case", title: "The hyperextended neck", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A woman in her mid-thirties with schizophrenia sat with her head wrenched back, wincing, refusing to answer for ten minutes as the neck extended further. A second clinician introduced himself as a physician, said he could see she was in pain, that he suspected a medication side effect, that she would feel his hand on the back of her head as he tried to free the neck, and that she should tell him if she wanted him to stop. Narrating each step, he had her neck straight within a minute and the interview continued, disorganized though her speech was. The spasm turned out to be hysterical rather than a dystonic reaction; the approach was right either way." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p4", label: "Practice: recognizing akathisia" }]
    },

    {
      id: "voices",
      title: "Voices: what they are and how patients live with them",
      lede: "Prevalence, inside or outside the head, realness and malingering, illusions and functional hallucinations, and the relationship a patient forms with a voice.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Auditory hallucinations are false perceptions of sound, human or not, the layperson's synonym for madness and the clinician's hard sign, though as Chapter 11 showed they also occur in people with no psychopathology. The commonest form is a voice: of someone known or unknown, real (a relative, a politician, a celebrity) or imagined (a god, a demon, an angel), from single words to sentences, questions, several voices conversing or commenting, and voices the patient converses with. Non-verbal hallucinations occur too, grunts, machine noise, unrecognizable sounds, music. One of Shea's patients, a student with psychotic bipolar disorder, reliably heard an ice-cream truck's jingle about half an hour before his demonic voices descended, approaching and leaving exactly like the real thing, and learned to take a prn dose of his antipsychotic the moment he heard it, often heading the demons off; each person must work out how to interact with their own hallucinations. Roughly 75% of people with schizophrenia hear voices, 20 to 50% with bipolar disorder, about 10% with major depression, and up to 40% with PTSD, mostly during flashbacks." },
        { type: "p", text: "Because you depend on self-report, start with the basic question of whether the voices are heard inside or outside the head, and expect a surprise. Jaspers divided pseudohallucinations from true hallucinations, holding that a true one had to seem substantial and real and to occupy space outside the head, with no continuum between hearing one's thoughts and true hallucination. Fish and modern experience showed him wrong: many patients with schizophrenia hear voices in their heads, voices sometimes move out into space as the psychosis advances and seem more real there, and both kinds can feel entirely real. The DSM-5 accepts both. It helps to think of location as within the mind (no place) versus outside the mind (a place can be assigned), and that place may itself be inside the body, like a transmitter implanted in the skull, or at the ear, on the skin, or anywhere in space. Copolov's group found about a third of patients heard voices inside the head, a third outside, and a third both, with no difference in severity or distress; internal voices went with somewhat better reality testing, yet, counter-intuitively, patients who heard commands only externally resisted them better. A diagnostic footnote: dissociative identity disorder patients may hear their alters internally, but internal voices make DID unlikely and schizophrenia more likely, and DID voices are not surrounded by delusional mood, delusional perception, or other signs of a budding psychosis." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Typical psychotic voices", items: [
            "Veridical, felt as real, yet on careful questioning often not exactly like normal voices; patients can frequently identify them as abnormal and may name them",
            "Emerge from subtle prodromal phases of delusional mood and perception",
            "Frequently hostile, hurling nasty or obscene insults",
            "Distance (insight) varies from patient to patient; explore what 'they sound real' means"
          ] },
          b: { title: "Red flags for malingering", items: [
            "Voices described as exactly like normal voices (possible in psychosis, not typical)",
            "Voices that started all of a sudden, out of nowhere",
            "A 'yes' to: on a really good day when you've been feeling fine, have the voices ever just started in on you out of nowhere?",
            "Absence of the hostile quality"
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "Two distinctions parallel the visual ones. An [[Auditory illusion]] distorts a real sound, as when a paranoid patient hears a friend's reassurance about being on time as a threat to slit his throat; it can be as frightening and as dangerous as a hallucination. A [[Functional hallucination]] is triggered by an external sound and heard alongside it, both clearly and without distortion: the phone rings and the neighbor's denigrating voice comes through the wall, the ring merely functioning as the trigger." },
        { type: "p", text: "Voices are phenomenologically unique to each person. Stephane and colleagues mapped twenty dimensions, which Shea tabulates from Waters: acoustic clarity (clear like external speech versus deep like thinking in words), personification (man, woman, robot, alien), loudness; location (head, other body parts, outer space); number; direction (talking among themselves or to the patient); syntax (first, second, or third person) and complexity (words, sentences, conversations); content range (repetitive versus systematized) and focus (self versus non-self); order (hearing, talking back, conversing); replay of things actually heard, or arising from the patient's own speech or thoughts; source attribution (self, a familiar other, God or a spirit, the dead); constant versus episodic, worsening versus improving; spontaneous versus triggered, and what the patient is doing when they come; what controls them (speech, non-speech sound, attention-demanding activity); and affective relatedness, comforting versus intrusive. Three of Schneider's first-rank symptoms add detail: [[Audible thoughts]] (hearing a thought just after thinking it, like an internal echo, or an undecipherable voice whose content becomes clear seconds later), voices arguing, and voices commenting on one's actions." },
        { type: "p", text: "Patients have their own urgent questions about each voice, which has its demands and expectations like a family member or friend: is it real; who or what makes it; does it mean me harm or good; will it leave when I want, or am I stuck with it; do others hear it; can it read my thoughts; does it want something of me; must I obey? The answers coalesce, as Waters puts it, into a relationship as important as any with real people, because the content is personalized, voices speak the person's fears and feelings, and they are taken for real entities. Shea quotes a first encounter: a man on a Coney Island boardwalk hears a deep, persistent voice telling him he is worthless and should walk into the ocean, tries to ignore it, hears it again louder and right in his ear, and knows from its cold command that he must obey. Exploring a patient's voices thoroughly and sensitively deepens engagement, because the patient feels met as an individual rather than processed as a case, and changes in their phenomenology (quieter, less frequent, moving inward, more distance) are often the earliest evidence that a medication or a coping technique is working. There is no better teacher than asking many people, psychotic and not, about their voices until you know the range." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch12", label: "Quiz: Chapter 12" }]
    },

    {
      id: "raising-voices",
      title: "Raising voices and exploring them",
      lede: "Two situations, the questions for each, the phenomenology list, and Kenney.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "When you decide to raise hallucinations you are in one of two situations: the patient has shown no sign of psychosis and you are screening, or psychotic material is already on the table. Screen everyone. Psychosis fluctuates even in schizophrenia, micropsychotic episodes in borderline, schizotypal, paranoid, narcissistic, and histrionic patients may leave no trace in a given hour, and a patient who looks intact may not be. The art is raising the topic without disengaging the patient." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Shea's favorite, demonstrated in Video Module 4.1: when you are feeling very depressed (anxious, stressed, upset), do your thoughts ever get so intense that they sound almost like a voice to you? It ties the phenomenon to the patient's pain, and 'like a voice' leaves a back door for someone who fears being thought crazy ('not really a voice, but sort of'), which further inquiry often turns into hallucinations.",
          "The SCAN question: do you ever seem to hear noises or voices when there is nobody about, and no ordinary explanation seems possible?",
          "When a delusion is already in play, tie the question to it: do you ever hear Fred's voice when he is not actually in the room with you? Does Fred ever try to talk with you or direct your thoughts from his house, or when you are at work?"
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Once raised, explore phenomenologically, choosing from the list in whatever order feels natural and spreading it over sessions: what the voices sound like; whether they sound just like real voices and how the patient tells the difference; inside the head, outside, or both (offer all three as things other patients report); loud or soft; how often; male, female, or other; recognized or not; named or not; what they say; whether they taunt or say mean things, and whether they ever say nice things; whether they tell the patient to hurt themselves (watching for self-mutilation such as enucleation or castration), to kill themselves, or to hurt or kill someone else; whether the patient thinks they are real or imagined; whether they wish them to stay or go; what they do to stop them; what they first thought the voices were; what they feel while hearing them; and whether several voices talk to each other about the patient. You cannot ask all of this in a first interview, but once voices are present you must always ask about commands, and if you become the treating clinician the list is a doorway to the patient's inner world for months to come." },
        { type: "case", title: "Kenney", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A college student in summer school walked into an assessment center saying he felt uptight and might benefit from counseling again. For fifteen minutes he was any anxious, overworked student, overwhelmed by school and terrified of dating. Shea then tapped an odd phrase from earlier, that the pressure to achieve felt like a guy inside him pushing all the time, expecting nothing and merely checking a soft sign (the intensity of the anxiety). The question opened a powder keg: a delusion of possession by a demon. Asked whether he heard the demon's voice, Kenney said not in his ears but somehow; it sounded like his thoughts but a little different; it just came to him. Asked whether the voice told him to hurt himself, he said yes: it told him not to study and to do badly on the test, because once he failed it would be easier for the demon to talk to him and harder not to listen, and it got louder when he failed. Only the specific question about cutting himself or taking pills reached the whispered command, 'do it'. His punitive superego had flavored the hallucination, and the dangerous commands surfaced only on specific questioning." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "From 'like a voice' to the voice", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "When the stress is at its worst, do your thoughts ever get so intense that they sound almost like a voice?", tag: "the back-door question" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Not a voice exactly. More like... someone else's thoughts. In my head, but not mine." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Can you tell them apart from your own thoughts? How?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "They're colder. They come at me. Mine don't come at me." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "What do they say?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "That I should stop pretending. That people can see through me." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Do they ever tell you to do things?", tag: "commands, general" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Sometimes. To skip class. To stay in my room." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Do they ever tell you to hurt yourself, or to end your life?", tag: "commands, specific" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "(long pause) When I've messed up. Then it says to just finish it." }
        ], note: "Composed. 'Not a voice exactly' was the back door working; the clinician neither argued about whether it counted as a voice nor stopped at the benign commands." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p1", label: "Practice: raising voices gently" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c12p2", label: "Practice: asking about commands" }, { kind: "sim", id: "sim-voice", label: "Simulation: Another voice" }]
    },

    {
      id: "commands",
      title: "Command hallucinations and dangerousness",
      lede: "What the research does and does not show, and the four dimensions that decide how dangerous a command is.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "A [[Command hallucination]] is any voice that tells the patient to do something, from 'go for a walk' to 'kill yourself'; when violence is urged it may call for prompt, even immediate, hospitalization, and because patients seldom volunteer dangerous commands (Kenney did not) you must ask. Papers from the 1980s claimed little or no statistical link between commands and dangerous acts, but they relied on hospital charts that never recorded the phenomenological variables that matter, so no one knows where on the continuum those voices lay; Shea regards the analyses as meaningless. Shawyer's well-designed study showed that some patients do act on commands, and that acting is more likely with older age, a belief that the command is positive and will bring benefit (killing the neighbor will end poverty), and a command tightly bound to a developed delusional system; antipsychotic medication was protective. Junginger, interviewing patients in detail, found that eight of twenty with dangerous commands had acted on them. Better research may bring better guidelines, but violence is not only a statistical event; it is phenomenological, decided by a unique psyche at a unique moment, and patients are not aware of the statistical rules they are meant to follow." },
        { type: "p", text: "Commands are not present-or-absent. They vary in content, auditory quality, the patient's felt ability to resist, and emotional impact, and they run from innocuous to a voice hammering incessantly for violence. A 65-year-old veteran Shea met in a VA clinic answered the command question with a twinkle: the Devil had been telling him to kill himself since he was sixteen, he had not listened then, and he was not going to start now. Violent in content, minimal in concern. At the other end are loud, insistent, harassing voices that are dangerous." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Dimension", "What to explore", "Composed questions"], rows: [
          ["Content", "Benign ('shut the door', 'change your profile picture', 'don't answer this idiot's questions') to self-harm ('cut your eye out', 'pull the trigger') to harm of others ('push him in front of the subway'); swearing and viciousness raise the pressure to comply. Even benign commands are followed in later sessions, because content can advance.", "Do your voices ever tell you to do things? What do they tell you to do? Do they ever tell you to hurt yourself or kill yourself? To hurt or kill someone else?"],
          ["Auditory quality", "Loudness (often surprising), long repetition, high frequency, an incessant barrage, insistence; yet a whisper from a revered source may be enough", "How loud do the voices get? How often do you hear the commands? Does the voice repeat them over and over? Does it insist?"],
          ["Ability to resist", "Patients often have good insight; reassurance has limited reliability, but a patient's belief that they cannot control the urge, and the palpable fear of doing what they do not want to do, must be taken very seriously; such patients are often relieved to be admitted", "How far do you think you can stop yourself from doing what Satan asks? How worried are you that you will? Should I, you, or your family be worried? Have you done anything to stop yourself?"],
          ["Emotional impact", "The authority of the voice's owner (parent, spouse, president, pope, God, Satan, a demon) raises pressure, most of all when a delusion promises a greater good ('world peace if you slay your child'; Satan threatening to torture the baby forever unless the mother cuts her throat); a malevolent, denigrating tone wears resistance down, and a patient may kill themselves to escape the voice or for fear of giving in and hurting someone", "Whose voice is it? What does it think of you? What would happen if you did what it says? What would happen if you didn't?"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The follow-up about what the patient has done to stop themselves is doubly informative. A patient who has taped all the kitchen knives into a shoebox and hidden it in a hard-to-reach corner of the attic is showing you protective effort and a hint of safety; the same answer shows how real the voice is to them and how worried they are that they may act. Add alcohol, street drugs, or sleeplessness to a vicious, authoritative, delusion-bound command, and you have an imminently dangerous situation." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Taping the knives", lines: [
          { who: "Pt.", t: "He says I have to do it before Sunday. He says it every night now." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "How far do you think you can stop yourself from doing what he's asking?", tag: "ability to resist" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "I don't know anymore. He's louder than he was." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Should I, or your wife, be worried that you'll do it?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "(quietly) Yes." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Have you done anything to stop yourself?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "I gave my wife the car keys and the shotgun shells. I made her lock them in her mother's house." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "That took real effort, and it tells me how real this is for you. I think the safest place for you right now is here with us, and I'd like to talk about that.", tag: "the patient's fear taken seriously; hospitalization raised" }
        ], note: "Composed. The patient's own forecast that he could not resist, and the protective steps he had already taken, decided the disposition more than any statistic." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p3", label: "Practice: assessing ability to resist" }]
    },

    {
      id: "thought-and-gates",
      title: "Thought process, the agitated patient, and the gates into psychotic material",
      lede: "Racing and contagious thought, blocking, broken logic and when to stop reasoning, and how to scout for paranoia without anyone noticing.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Perception is not the only casualty. Thought speeds up and races as in mania; concentration fails; a paragraph takes ages because every line starts the mind in ten directions. Psychotic thinking has an internally contagious quality, triggering associations near and far that the patient does not control (an ashtray calls up a dozen connected things at once), which shows outwardly as a loosening of associations, [[Derailment|derailment]], examined in Chapter 16. Sometimes the process is disrupted outright: the patient stops mid-sentence and cannot return to the topic, [[Thought blocking]], a strongly suggestive sign of psychosis. Ask quietly what just happened; often a voice intervened, or a thought was withdrawn. Knowing whether the patient is hallucinating during the interview matters for another reason: they may think the clinician is producing the messages, and being perceived as calling the patient a drunk or threatening to chop off their fingers does nothing for rapport and is the state in which violence toward the interviewer can erupt." },
        { type: "p", text: "Logic itself breaks. Rosenbaum's predicative thinking ([[Paleologic]]) treats things as identical because they share a predicate: Jesus was persecuted; I am persecuted; therefore I am Jesus. Magical thinking of the kind seen in small children accompanies it. None of this is all or nothing; many patients retain varying degrees of normal logic, and that variability is immediately useful with a patient who is both psychotic and agitated. Do not decide beforehand whether they can be talked down. Engage gently, assess how intact the logic is as you go, and if it is reasonably intact, talk and try to ease the anger. If the anger escalates with your attempts, or the logic proves severely impaired, back off quickly and start the safety procedures; further reasoning makes no sense, because the patient is not processing your words normally, and pushing on can push toward violence. The same holds when you discover you have been incorporated into the delusion. Shea once sat down to observe an interview and was greeted by a ragged, hostile patient shouting that he was the one who had called her a prostitute and spied on her; every word in his defense made her angrier, so he stopped talking, which was both valor and sound technique, because she was not hearing him, and from her side he really had belittled her publicly. You cannot quickly detach yourself from a delusional web." },
        { type: "p", text: "With non-agitated patients, blending is far more available, and the question becomes how to broach paranoia and other psychotic topics without offending someone who has shown no sign of psychosis. The art is to key off the content of the conversation through the natural, implied, and referred gates of Chapter 4. A woman describing an ugly marriage after the mill closed can be asked whether the tension has ever led to him striking her, and then whether she thinks he is trying to hurt her in any way, perhaps turning her friends against her; her answer (he tries to make her feel guilty but knows better than to mess with her or her friends) suggests no paranoid ideation, most paranoid patients would have nibbled at the bait, and she has no idea that psychotic material was just scouted. Some authors advise never asking about voices and the like unless strongly suspicious, for fear of disengagement; Shea disagrees. The approach misses fluctuating and micropsychotic process and treatable disorders such as schizotypal personality; he has seldom seen lasting damage to engagement, most patients are not offended at all, and the handful who balk can be re-engaged. He therefore screens all his patients at some point." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "The patient who balked", lines: [
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Let's be clear, it's been hard on everybody and I've been upset. Who wouldn't be." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "When you're that upset, do your thoughts ever get so intense that they sound almost like a voice to you?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "(angrily) Here come the crazy questions. I'm not crazy, and I've been asked all this before. (squeezes her boyfriend's hand, smiles at him, sneers at the clinician)" },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "(gently) That wasn't meant as a judgment. It's something I ask everyone I meet. I'm sorry it landed badly.", tag: "repair; and a note to self" }
        ], note: "Composed after the chapter's reconstructed exchange. The over-reaction, unusual for this question, read as a narcissistic injury and opened a window on pain below the anger; it prompted gentler questions and a careful look at personality in the social history, where a borderline personality came into view. Even a balk is data." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Have you had experiences that seemed odd or frightening to you?",
          "Earlier we talked about your nightmares. Have similar frightening images ever bothered you during the day?",
          "You mentioned your talents. Have you ever felt you had unusual abilities, like ESP?",
          "You said you love watching TV or YouTube. Has it ever frightened you? Did it ever seem that the people on it were watching you, or knew private things about your life?",
          "On Twitter, do tweets from strangers or famous people ever seem to be about you, or aimed at you personally?",
          "You mentioned your sister hearing voices. Have you ever had anything similar?",
          "Religion is one of the best gates: how much is God part of your life moment to moment? People close to God sometimes feel He has a special role for them; do you feel you might be lucky enough that He has one for you? (The patient who answered that she was to bring peace to all nations had shown, by the intensity of her feeling, that something was up before the gate was even opened.)",
          "A few probes tell you whether to expand. If hints emerge, expand fully; if none, leave the region."
        ] }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p1", label: "Practice: raising voices gently" }]
    },

    {
      id: "self",
      title: "The wounded self and the dyadic wing",
      lede: "What deficit symptoms do to a person, recovery versus rehabilitation, the precox feeling, agreeing to disagree, and your own effect on the patient.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Of all the damage, Shea believes the damage to the self is the worst. The negative symptoms of schizophrenia (low interest, drive, and energy) linger long after voices and delusions fade, and almost every patient first reads them not as illness but as proof of personal inadequacy. Work becomes hard or impossible; self-belittlement feeds on itself; weary families call it laziness and wonder why, with the voices gone, he does not just get a job. Siblings and friends move on to marriages, careers, and identities, and the patient is left behind in a race that looks unwinnable, decides it is not worth the risk of further humiliation, and settles into the role of the family's black sheep. This demoralization is often worst between episodes, when the deficit symptoms persist, and it is frequently the prelude to suicide (Chapter 17). Shea's phrase is that schizophrenia rapes the soul; helping the patient believe in themselves again is a core task from the first interview on, and the people he has met coping with this illness are among the toughest and most courageous he knows. They are not to be pitied." },
        { type: "p", text: "Recovery begins with two realizations. For the clinician: we are all people first, the diagnosis is secondary, the person across from you is not a medical term, and you or your family could develop bipolar disorder or schizophrenia; mental illness respects no boundary of class, education, or income. For the patient: I am not a disease but a person coping with one. Hence abandon the word 'schizophrenic', which leads insidiously to stigma and identification with the disease; Shea has never met a schizophrenic but has met many people coping with schizophrenia. This respect shows in the first encounter and predicts who comes back to follow-up. Patricia Deegan's distinction frames it: [[Recovery vs rehabilitation|rehabilitation]] is the services and technologies offered so that a person can adapt; recovery is the lived experience of accepting and overcoming the disability and finding a new sense of self and purpose within and beyond its limits. People do not 'get rehabilitated' the way cars get tuned." },
        { type: "p", text: "In the dyadic wing, social withdrawal is extremely common in schizophrenia, partly autistic retreat to sort out the flood of odd sensations, partly fear of losing control as aggressive drives build. Patients also break the nonverbal rules of conversation (Chapter 8): standing or sitting too close, odd or restricted affect, and a strikingly diminished capacity to empathize because so much attention is turned inward. On your side, notice confusion. A clinician's confusion is often produced by the patient's subtle loosening or illogic and mirrors their disorganization; recognizing it should prompt a more thorough search. Intuitive clinicians fall into a trap here: they understand what the patient means and therefore overlook the thought disorder. The question is never whether you understand, but whether a non-professional sitting beside the patient on a bus would. All of this can produce the [[Precox feeling]], an inability to feel with the patient, an empathic bond that will not form, which is suggestive of schizophrenia and useful as an intuitive prompt to check the criteria, but never a criterion and never a justification for a label." },
        { type: "p", text: "Frustration is the other common reaction: patients without insight reject case management, therapy, medication, or admission, and the paranoid patient who plainly needs help insists nothing is wrong. Accept the frustration as natural and keep it off your face. The countertransference usually surfaces as long, heated attempts to convince the patient of their illness, which are far more counterproductive than productive. State your view calmly, then [[Agree to disagree]]: you will always say what you really believe and you know they will too; they are entitled to their opinion; you could be wrong; only time will sort it out; and the door is open whenever they want to talk again. Frustration also comes from the sense that a patient switches the psychosis on when it suits them. Shea recalls a man whose speech organized as he grew comfortable and then dissolved into talk of nuclear warheads over Pittsburgh the moment his personal life was probed, probably without conscious intent; to the extent patients have insight and motivation they may partly rein the process in, hiding embarrassing material takes enormous effort, and sometimes it is easier to let go. Understanding that lowers your frustration. With persistent negativism, Michels suggests gently pointing out that automatic 'no' relinquishes control exactly as automatic 'yes' would, and agreeing jointly on a topic can open engagement." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "You are in the system too", text: "Chapter 1's advice stands: with paranoid patients, lower the empathic valence and avoid implied certainty or intuited attribution. Ordinary words can carry private meanings (Michels's patient for whom 'leg' had become sexual). Frightened, cooperative patients are helped by hearing that they are safe; frightened and disorganized ones by being told what is happening, invited to ask questions, and given structure, because an unstructured interview of open questions and pregnant pauses can itself be traumatizing, and gentle structure sometimes organizes the speech as psychotic defenses recede. And an empathic interviewer can lower a subtly psychotic patient's anxiety so far that the psychosis temporarily disappears, distorting the picture you came to see. Family and friends are caught in the same system, untrained." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p5", label: "Practice: agreeing to disagree" }]
    },

    {
      id: "families",
      title: "The family wing: mourning, denial, alliance, and confidentiality",
      lede: "What psychosis does to a family, how to win parents in the first meeting, and how to handle the question of who may be told what.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Psychosis, for all its withdrawal, is a family matter; no one nearby stays uninvolved for long, and few processes so ravage a family's structure, above all when the illness is chronic. The pain of watching a child or spouse tortured by psychosis, and then learning it may last years or a lifetime, destroys the dreams of patient and family alike. Damaging matrix effects follow: financial ruin, an adult child living at home, stigma from culture and friends, and two demoralizing processes almost every parent faces, a numbing helplessness and overwhelming caretaker fatigue. Relatives may become the objects of penetrating hatred once they are written into a delusion; some are assaulted, a few killed by those who loved them most. They juggle embarrassment, guilt, fear, compassion, helplessness, bitterness, love, and the wish to abandon the patient. Families are therefore both an invaluable source of information and a target for healing by the team, and many readers of the book, Shea notes, already know this pain from a loved one, or will." },
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Jenkins", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A Creole woman in her mid-thirties, elegant in a faded scarf and defiant of jaw, had grown progressively depressed with religious delusions, stopped working, and moved in with her mother and a brother, who cared for her children and felt she would recover with God's help rather than professional help. Another brother angrily insisted on treatment; the tension over 'what to do with Sandy' was already splitting the family. In the emergency department waiting room she rose and began a ritual chant while her mother and brother, hiding their embarrassment, coaxed her back to her seat. Later the same mother sincerely thanked the staff and declined admission: nothing much was wrong, she would pull out of it. The next day they were back after a night of bizarre behavior, the mother's arm around her daughter and her eyes red with the recognition that this was no longer the person she had raised. Schizoaffective disorder had shifted the matrix, perhaps for good." },
        { type: "p", text: "Families mourn the person they knew, and the stages of mourning (denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance) intermingle and recur. The Jenkins family shows the commonest problem the initial interviewer meets, a powerful system of denial, and understanding it as mourning softens the angry countertransference that a parent's rejection of help can provoke. The family warrants assessment early, and later counseling; some relatives become seriously depressed or suicidal, a damaging matrix effect in its own right. Family tension can also aggravate the psychosis: the EPICS project found that families over-involved with the patient, hostilely or in an overly concerned caring way, hinder recovery even when the patient takes medication, and that family counseling significantly cuts relapse. As the first professional the family meets, you are the key person in starting that alliance. Friends and jobs fall away too, and friends carry guilt; a simple early remark that you can see how much they have been through and how good it was of them to come goes a long way." },
        { type: "p", text: "Many parents, siblings, and children arrive with a checkered history with mental health professionals, having been put through the wringer by the disease and sometimes by the people treating it. Winning them over in the first meeting is critical. Murray-Swank and colleagues (Appendix IV) advise meeting the family where they are: thank them, ask whether you may hear their thoughts about the problems that brought their relative to treatment, then what they think caused them, whether anyone has given a diagnosis (what they understand it to mean, and whether they agree), what makes things better, and what makes things worse. Their answers guide treatment, and families often know the prodromal signs of relapse better than anyone." },
        { type: "table", head: ["The parent's unspoken fear", "What to say (composed, after the chapter)"], rows: [
          ["This clinician doesn't care what I have to say", "Nobody in the world knows him better than you. We depend on your input, especially on what has worked and what hasn't."],
          ["This clinician thinks we are the problem", "Some people think parents cause schizophrenia. I don't believe that for a minute; it is a brain disease, like epilepsy. Some of the most loving parents I know have children with it, and watching you today I can see how much you love him. He is lucky to have you."],
          ["He won't understand what we've been through", "You must have been through so much, and it must feel as if it never ends. I have no miracles, but I promise we will do our best for you and your son. How have you coped? Have you had much support?"],
          ["This 'Bozo' is going to change the meds", "Changing medication before hearing from the family and the patient about what has worked would be foolish. I won't recommend anything until we've talked in detail, and if your son agrees I'd like to hear from you before any major change. What do you think of the current regimen?"],
          ["He's closed to our ideas and thinks he knows it all", "If you come across articles or hear of treatments, tell me or the case manager; I'll look into anything I don't know. I like to keep up."]
        ] },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Ask early what their experience with previous psychiatrists (or your discipline) has been like, and what you could do that would help them, including how often they would like to meet. The asking metacommunicates that their view of your care matters.",
          "Offer support beyond the clinic: the local NAMI chapter (or its equivalent elsewhere), and family guides such as Mueser's Complete Family Guide to Schizophrenia and Torrey's Surviving Schizophrenia.",
          "Take a read on the family's emotional state at admission, especially on a locked unit; acknowledge that they may be anxious, overwhelmed, angry, or all at once, and that this is normal.",
          "A parent who has just committed a child involuntarily, and faced that child's rage and sense of betrayal, is besieged by guilt. Say that it was the right thing, that it may have saved a life, that it took courage and love, and that the team will do everything it can.",
          "Mueser and Glynn's three principles: let the family know they are not alone; provide support and let them vent; instill hope."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "With adult patients you need the patient's permission before contacting relatives, and psychosis has often strained those relationships through nobody's fault. Murray-Swank's approach: ask who the patient considers family or like family (friends, a pastor, an AA or NA sponsor count), whether they live together or how close, how often they talk, text, message, or meet, whether these people have been supportive through the illness (using summaries and reflections to help the patient feel supported), whether they have been involved in care or attended educational programs before, whether the patient would like some or all of them involved now, what the benefits might be, and what concerns there are. The goals are to identify allies, consider the advantages, and surface the worries, so that an ambivalent patient (given complex histories and sometimes abusive relatives) makes an informed choice. Shea's own view is that open channels with caring relatives are an immense comfort, and that being told nothing 'because of confidentiality' is frustrating and frightening for a family. If permission is withheld, soften the exclusion with Havens's [[Counterprojection]]: medical information is protected, you cannot share specifics without her permission, it is hard for families in this situation and hard for you too because you value including families, and you will ask her for permission at the next chance. When suicide or violence is a concern, the need for a sound risk assessment trumps confidentiality; family information can be life-saving; consult a supervisor or colleague if at all possible and document the reasons for the override." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Who is family to you?", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "I'd like to understand your support system. Are there people you'd consider family, or like family? Who would they be?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "My kuya. And Pastor Ben, from church. Not my parents." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Your brother and your pastor. Have they been supportive while you've been dealing with all this?" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Kuya drives me here. Pastor Ben prays with me. He doesn't believe in the medicine, though." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Would you want either of them involved in your care here? What might be good about that, and what worries you about it?", tag: "benefits and concerns, so the choice is informed" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Kuya, yes. Pastor Ben... I don't want him told about the voices." }
        ], note: "Composed. Family is whom the patient names; the pastor's view of medication is treatment-relevant information; and the patient's limits on what is shared are respected and recorded." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p6", label: "Practice: addressing a family's fears" }]
    },

    {
      id: "culture-language",
      title: "Culture, subculture, and language",
      lede: "Missing a culturally specific psychosis, mistaking a cultural norm for one, the clinic's own subculture, and a Filipino patient whose delusions lived in Ilocano.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Psychosis takes different shapes under the matrix effects of different cultures, the presentations often called [[Culture-bound syndrome|culture-bound syndromes]], a term Shea hopes will be retired because every diagnosis is culture-bound, the DSM-5's included; it would be more accurate to say these syndromes are not found in Western culture except in immigrants and visitors. The lesson is that cultures shape psychosis, so the interviewer must know the patient's culture of origin and watch for variations, or risk both errors: missing a psychosis and misreading a normal experience as one." },
        { type: "case", title: "The graduate student's father", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A Chinese graduate student at an American university brought in his visiting father, who had been wound up for two weeks, locking himself in the guest room at night and growing angry when his son knocked. The 55-year-old was friendly but hesitant, admitted anxiety and marked sleep trouble, showed unusually intense affect, and was mortified to admit yelling at his son. Reading the soft signs correctly, the clinician asked delicately about voices, paranoia, delusions, alien control, and hyper-religiosity, and found nothing; he concluded that anxiety, social phobia, or a hidden situational stress was likelier. All reasonable, all wrong. The question he did not know to ask, framed as a normalization about private fears of the body and the worry that the penis is being damaged or drawn into the body, even fatally, produced a shy nod. [[Koro]] (shuk yang, suo yang, jinjinia bemar, rok-joo by region; in women a feared retraction of the vulva or nipples) would never have been volunteered, and the father could have left without anyone knowing." },
        { type: "p", text: "Mezzich's case of qi-gong-induced psychosis makes the same point from the other side: a 28-year-old Chinese man in the United States developed weeks of delusions that his qi was circulating the wrong way and voices of supernatural beings instructing his practice, after taking up qi-gong for back pain and exhaustion; medical work-up was normal, haloperidol helped, and the picture meets criteria in the Chinese classification system, where brief psychotic reactions to the practice are familiar. Other culturally specific syndromes are psychotic (locura), sometimes psychotic (amok, iich'aa, bouffée délirante, taijin kyofusho), or non-psychotic (bilis, colera, muina, kufungisisa, hwa-byung), and the DSM appendices describe them; the point is awareness, not a catalogue." },
        { type: "p", text: "The opposite error is the [[Kulturbrille]] effect of Chapter 6, and Shea's example is a new graduate from the Southwest hired in Minneapolis and soon sitting with a Hmong immigrant. The Hmong, many of them war refugees, carry years of camps, trauma, poverty, relocation, and lost networks and roles, and mental health problems are high (half of Cambodian refugees were depressed after twenty years in the United States). Most Hmong have a word for severe distress, nyuaj siab, but none for depression as the West defines it, and they attribute nyuaj siab and much illness to spirits and ghosts. Many non-Western cultures draw the line between real and imagined less sharply than the West does, so a Hmong patient may speak matter-of-factly of seeing spirits and hearing their voices; a clinician who hears psychosis in that is wearing the kulturbrille. Some such patients are psychotic, and the way to tell is the one from Chapter 11: psychotic voices sit in a matrix of delusional mood, delusional perception, and the soft signs; cultural voices do not." },
        { type: "p", text: "There is also a subculture nobody mentions: the one patients with chronic illness join as friends fall away, hospital stays lengthen, and relatives' welcome wears out. They spend their time with each other, at the community mental health center, clubhouses, patient-run centers, and residences, while society's stigma, fear, and ignorance push them into a caste of outcasts, which can surface as veiled or open hostility toward you. This subculture has strong views about treatment, including prejudices about particular drugs; if all of a patient's friends hate risperidone, sending him home on it makes little sense when an equally effective alternative is not blackballed. Ask whether the patient knows anyone around here taking the medication you have in mind, what that person thinks of it, and what patients in general here think of it. One of Shea's patients explained that paroxetine caused 'Paxil-head', a wound-up feeling with its own local name; paroxetine was clearly not the choice." },
        { type: "case", title: "Delusions only in Ilocano", text: "Summarized from the chapter, from Junji Takeshita of the University of Hawaii. A 79-year-old Filipino man was admitted through the courts after deciding to kill his wife before she, as he believed, killed him. Interviewed in English by a non-Filipino psychiatrist, he showed no delusions or odd beliefs, cooperated, and was a model patient, though the psychiatrist felt his limited English constrained the interview. Filipino nurses who interpreted found him fluent in Ilocano and much weaker in Tagalog. In Ilocano he elaborated fixed, extensive delusions that multiple family members were trying to kill him; in Tagalog only fragments of paranoid thought emerged; in English, nothing." },
        { type: "p", text: "Interpreters, and bilingual clinicians, are precious and scarce, and Takeshita's case shows where to spend them: on uncovering psychotic process. When you suspect psychosis in a patient whose language you do not speak fluently, strongly consider a re-interview with an interpreter who speaks the patient's exact language, or as close as possible, because even the difference between two languages of the same country decided whether lethal delusions were shared. Takeshita adds that bilingual patients may lose their second language during a psychotic episode, regressing to the first as disorganization grows. Language also carries stigma: where serious mental illness is a family failure, it is alluded to in euphemisms, and a clinician who does not know them misses major disorders in the past psychiatric and family history (in Japan, 'neurasthenia' and shinshinsho, a psychosomatic label, can stand in for schizophrenia). Among white Americans, 'nervous breakdown' in a family history usually means at least an agitated major depression, at most a psychotic or manic episode, and often a hospitalization; ask what it involved. Relatives may also simply hide serious disorders when giving a family history." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "For a Philippine service", text: "Takeshita's patient is a local lesson. A patient from the Ilocos, the Visayas, or Mindanao interviewed in Tagalog or English may be as mute about delusions as this man was in English. Ask early which language the patient thinks and dreams in, interview in it when you can, find a nurse or relative who speaks it when you cannot, and treat a delusion-free interview in a second language as inconclusive when the soft signs say otherwise." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch12", label: "Quiz: Chapter 12" }]
    },

    {
      id: "meaning",
      title: "The worldview wing: a world too meaningful, and Jake",
      lede: "Religious preoccupation that stops being a support, the delusion that props up a self, and the person beneath every delusion.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Through the window of psychosis the problem is not that the world is meaningless but that it is too meaningful: a suffocating mix of bizarre, unscreened sensations turns it into a desert full of burning bushes, with no rest from the intensity, which is the source of the driven quality of psychotic process. One of the saddest ironies is that religious preoccupation can grow so intense that religion stops working as a support; instead of calm guidance it becomes a source of disturbance, a pattern common in schizophrenia and mania. Shea suggests that psychotic religiosity may be an unconscious replacement for lost sources of meaning: when family ties have frayed and the patient feels like a burden, a grandiose religious delusion offers solace, a resurrection of self-esteem. For some patients the delusions become the focal point of life, and when the delusions go, so can the meaning." },
        { type: "case", title: "Jake", text: "Summarized from the chapter. Jake, lean and wispy-haired, spoke with a child's innocence and carried an adult's burdens: married to a tough, loving, pregnant seventeen-year-old, both unemployed and unskilled, about to lose their housing, estranged from their families, with the social wing of his matrix in ruins, and he blamed himself. Admitted in a severe first break of schizophrenia, he was tormented by demons of judgment and persecutory voices. One delusion he welcomed: that he could broadcast his thoughts on purpose, faster and farther than anyone. As the psychosis lifted and the terrible delusions faded, that one faded too. One evening, out of sorts at his bedside, he asked Shea, with something like fright in his eyes, whether he could really send his thoughts out; told gently that this belief was probably part of the schizophrenia too, and that Shea was sorry it was not true for him, Jake wept. The delusion had been the last prop of a battered ego. Years later Shea heard that Jake had died by a self-inflicted gunshot." },
        { type: "p", text: "Jake's lesson is to understand what a belief means to the patient at the moment it is presented, because there is always a person beneath the psychosis, beneath every delusion. Even when the psychosis is primarily biological, delusional content is drawn from the person's upbringing, memories, values, culture, and spirituality, so these illogical fantasies hold clues to underlying fears, strengths, and issues. And chronic severe mental illness brings a quiet desperation as dreams are shattered, intensified by the guilt and shame of 'having become a failure' that a stigmatizing culture reinforces, until patients lose the belief that their existence has purpose. The collapse of that spiritual support is one of the precipitants of the suicidal ideation so often seen in severe psychotic illness, as it was for Jake." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Back to de Nerval", text: "Shea closes the two psychosis chapters where they began, with the poet found hanging in a Paris alley, and asks the reader to re-read his description of a world in which nature spoke to him and his companions' words carried messages only he understood, now with respect both for its brilliance and for the pain that brilliance echoes." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c12p5", label: "Practice: agreeing to disagree" }]
    }
  ],

  deeper: [
    { title: "Read in the book", text: "The patients' own descriptions quoted by Shea and by Mellor (limbs on strings, made unhappiness, the puppet, thought leakage for miles, the first voice on the boardwalk, racing and contagious thought); the full direct transcript of the young professional describing akathisia; Table 12.1, Stephane's twenty dimensions of auditory verbal hallucinations; the complete list of phenomenology questions; the Kenney transcript; Robinson-style and Murray-Swank wording for families; and the koro question in full." },
    { title: "Appendix IV and Video Module 4.1", text: "Murray-Swank, Dixon, and Stewart's article on building an alliance with the families of patients with severe mental illness, reprinted in Appendix IV and drawn on heavily in this chapter's family section. Video Module 4.1 (facilics) demonstrates Shea's way of raising auditory hallucinations; Video Module 16.2 shows the attention tests from Chapter 11." },
    { title: "Chapters this one connects to", text: "Chapter 11 for the life cycle of a psychosis, the psychotic matrix, soft signs, and tapping odd phrases (the Kenney interview applies it); Chapter 1 for empathic valence with paranoid patients; Chapter 4 for natural, implied, and referred gates; Chapter 6 for the kulturbrille; Chapter 8 for nonverbal disturbance; Chapter 10 for the family as co-sufferer; Chapter 16 for derailment and thought blocking in the mental status; Chapter 17 for suicide in chronic psychotic illness; Chapter 18 for violence." },
    { title: "Sources Shea draws on", text: "Kurt Schneider and Jaspers on first-rank symptoms and the phenomenology of hallucination; Mellor's classic paper on first-rank symptoms; Fish; Waters's overview of auditory hallucinations and Stephane's phenomenological structure; Copolov on the location of voices; Shawyer and Junginger on command hallucinations; Rosenbaum on predicative thinking; Michels on negativism and private word meanings; Patricia Deegan on recovery; the EPICS project on family involvement; Murray-Swank, Mueser and Glynn, Mueser's and Torrey's family guides, and NAMI; Leston Havens on counterprojection; Mezzich, Lee, Al-Issa, and Takeshita on culture and language; and the culture-bound syndrome appendices of the DSM-IV-TR and DSM-5." }
  ]
};
